data.reasoningText:
Now I have a clear picture. Let me plan the component decomposition and then implement it.

## Component Plan: Settings Dashboard

### Component Tree
```
Settings                    (page — owns active section, orchestrates layout)
├── SettingsSidebar         (vertical nav tabs)
├── ProfileSection          (profile editing form)
├── NotificationsSection    (notification toggles and channel muting)
├── SecuritySection         (password, 2FA, sessions)
│   ├── ChangePasswordPanel (expandable password change form)
│   └── ActiveSessionsTable (sessions list with revoke)
└── AppearanceSection       (theme, font, language, compact mode + preview)


```

For the data flow, I'll have the Settings page manage which section is active, but each section will inject the service directly rather than having the page orchestrate everything—that keeps things cleaner and more modular. The sidebar just needs to know the current section and a callback to switch between them.

I'll start by setting up the models and service interface, then build out the sidebar, followed by each section component in order.

```

I'm organizing the project structure with a Services folder for the models and interface, and all the settings components under Components/Pages/Settings/. I'll need to create the Razor components for each section along with their styling files, plus the main Settings page that ties everything together.

My approach is to build the service layer first, then work through the leaf components like the sidebar and password panel, move up to the section components, and finally create the main page component. This bottom-up approach ensures dependencies are in place as I build.

```

csharp
namespace sv_0337a3558cd14350832d009a0927d52d.Models
{
    public class UserProfile
    {
        public string Id { get; set; }
        public string Username { get; set; }
        public string Email { get; set; }
        public string FullName { get; set; }
        public string AvatarUrl { get; set; }
        public DateTime CreatedAt { get; set; }
    }

    public class NotificationSettings
    {
        public bool EmailNotifications { get; set; }
        public bool PushNotifications { get; set; }
        public bool MarketingEmails { get; set; }
    }

    public class AppearanceSettings
    {
        public string Theme { get; set; }
        public string Language { get; set; }
        public bool CompactMode { get; set; }
    }

    public class ActiveSession
    {
        public string Id { get; set; }
        public string DeviceName { get; set; }
        public string IpAddress { get; set; }
        public DateTime LastActive { get; set; }
        public bool IsCurrent { get; set; }
    }
}
```

Now I'll create the service interface and implementation to handle all the settings operations.

